This is a classic bitmask DP problem. With n up to 7, we have up to 14 elements, so 2^14 = 16384 states. We use memoization over the mask of used elements and the operation number (which is determined by the number of used elements: operation index = popcount(mask)/2 + 1).

Approach: DP over mask. For each mask, the number of operations done = popcount(mask)/2. The next operation index = popcount(mask)/2 + 1. We pick the first unset bit i, then try pairing it with every other unset bit j, computing score = opIndex * gcd(nums[i], nums[j]) + dp(mask | (1<<i) | (1<<j)).

Memoize with an array of size 1 << (2n), initialized to -1.

Let me write the C# code:

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int MaxScore(int[] nums) {
        int m = nums.Length;
        int full = (1 << m) - 1;
        var memo = new int[1 << m];
        Array.Fill(memo, -1);

        int Gcd(int a, int b) {
            while (b != 0) {
                int t = a % b;
                a = b;
                b = t;
            }
            return a;
        }

        int Dp(int mask) {
            if (mask == full) return 0;
            if (memo[mask] != -1) return memo[mask];
            int ops = BitOperations.PopCount(mask) / 2; // need using System.Numerics
            int opIndex = ops + 1;
            int best = 0;
            // find first unset bit
            int i = 0;
            while ((mask & (1 << i)) != 0) i++;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < m; j++) {
                if ((mask & (1 << j)) == 0) {
                    int score = opIndex * Gcd(nums[i], nums[j]) + Dp(mask | (1 << i) | (1 << j));
                    if (score > best) best = score;
                }
            }
            memo[mask] = best;
            return best;
        }

        return Dp(0);
    }
}
```

