The problem: find eventual safe states. Classic approach: reverse graph + topological sort (Kahn's algorithm), or DFS with coloring (detect cycles; nodes not in a cycle and not leading to a cycle are safe).

Approach 1: DFS with three colors:
- 0 = unvisited, 1 = in progress (visiting), 2 = safe.
- If we encounter a node in "visiting" state, it's part of a cycle → unsafe.
- Node is safe if all its neighbors are safe.

With n up to 10^4 and edges up to 4*10^4, recursion depth could be up to 10^4 which might be fine in C# (default stack ~1MB, each frame small). But to be safe, iterative DFS or BFS on reversed graph is better.

Approach 2: Reverse graph + outdegree. A node is safe if all paths lead to terminal nodes. Terminal nodes have outdegree 0. Kahn's algorithm on reversed graph: start with nodes with outdegree 0 (terminal). Process: for each terminal node, decrement outdegree of its predecessors (in reversed graph, its neighbors). When a predecessor's outdegree reaches 0, it becomes safe and is added to queue. Finally, all nodes with outdegree 0 (original outdegree reduced to 0) are safe. Collect and sort (they'll naturally be in ascending order if we iterate i from 0 to n-1).

Let me implement:

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public IList<int> EventualSafeNodes(int[][] graph) {
        int n = graph.Length;
        var reverseAdj = new List<int>[n];
        int[] outDegree = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            reverseAdj[i] = new List<int>();
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            outDegree[i] = graph[i].Length;
            foreach (int next in graph[i]) {
                reverseAdj[next].Add(i);
            }
        }
        var queue = new Queue<int>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (outDegree[i] == 0) queue.Enqueue(i);
        }
        while (queue.Count > 0) {
            int node = queue.Dequeue();
            foreach (int prev in reverseAdj[node]) {
                outDegree[prev]--;
                if (outDegree[prev] == 0) queue.Enqueue(prev);
            }
        }
        var result = new List<int>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (outDegree[i] == 0) result.Add(i);
        }
        return result;
    }
}
```

