Looking at this problem:

**Approach:**
1. Sort gardens ascending. In an optimal solution, the gardens made complete should be the ones with the largest initial counts (exchange argument shows this minimizes total cost for any target "k complete + min level v").
2. For each `k` (number of complete gardens = top `k` of sorted array), compute the cost to raise them to `target`. If affordable, use the remaining budget to maximize the minimum of the remaining `m = n - k` gardens (a "water filling" problem), capped at `target - 1` (since a garden reaching `target` becomes complete).
3. The max minimum is found by binary searching the level `v`: cost to raise all first `m` gardens to `v` is `j*v - prefix[j]` where `j` is the count of gardens below `v` (found via binary search on the sorted prefix).
4. Beauty candidate = `k*full + v*partial`; if `m == 0`, candidate = `n*full`. Since cost is non-decreasing in `k`, we can break early. Candidates where the "incomplete" set contains already-complete gardens are dominated by larger `k`, so they never overestimate.

Verified against both examples (14 and 30) and several edge cases (all gardens already complete, target=1, single garden, etc.). Complexity: O(n log² n), well within limits; all arithmetic fits in `long` (values up to ~2×10¹⁰).

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution {
    public long MaximumBeauty(int[] flowers, long newFlowers, int target, int full, int partial) {
        int n = flowers.Length;
        Array.Sort(flowers);

        long[] prefix = new long[n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + flowers[i];

        long ans = 0;
        long costComplete = 0;

        for (int k = 0; k <= n; k++) {
            if (k > 0) {
                int d = target - flowers[n - k];
                if (d > 0) costComplete += d;
                if (costComplete > newFlowers) break;
            }

            long rem = newFlowers - costComplete;
            int m = n - k;

            if (m == 0) {
                long candAll = (long)n * full;
                if (candAll > ans) ans = candAll;
                continue;
            }

            if (flowers[0] >= target) continue;

            long lo = flowers[0];
            long hi = (long)target - 1;
            while (lo < hi) {
                long mid = lo + ((hi - lo + 1) >> 1);
                int bLo = 0, bHi = m;
                while (bLo < bHi) {
                    int bMid = (bLo + bHi) >> 1;
                    if (flowers[bMid] < mid) bLo = bMid + 1;
                    else bHi = bMid;
                }
                long need = (long)bLo * mid - prefix[bLo];
                if (need <= rem) lo = mid;
                else hi = mid - 1;
            }

            long cand = (long)k * full + lo * partial;
            if (cand > ans) ans = cand;
        }

        return ans;
    }
}
```

