This is a classic binary search on the answer problem. We binary search over the number of days. For each candidate day, check if we can make m bouquets of k adjacent bloomed flowers. The check is O(n): scan through the array, count consecutive bloomed flowers, when count reaches k, make a bouquet and reset count.

Edge case: if (long)m * k > n, return -1 to avoid overflow issues (m up to 10^6, k up to n up to 10^5, so m*k up to 10^11, needs long).

Binary search bounds: low = min bloomDay (or 1), high = max bloomDay (or 10^9).

The check function: given day d, count how many bouquets possible. Iterate: if bloomDay[i] <= d, streak++; else streak = 0. If streak == k, bouquets++, streak = 0. Return bouquets >= m.

Let me write the code:

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int MinDays(int[] bloomDay, int m, int k) {
        long need = (long)m * k;
        if (need > bloomDay.Length) return -1;

        int lo = 1, hi = 0;
        foreach (var d in bloomDay) if (d > hi) hi = d;

        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            if (CanMake(bloomDay, mid, m, k)) hi = mid;
            else lo = mid + 1;
        }
        return lo;
    }

    private bool CanMake(int[] bloomDay, int day, int m, int k) {
        int bouquets = 0, streak = 0;
        foreach (var d in bloomDay) {
            if (d <= day) {
                streak++;
                if (streak == k) {
                    bouquets++;
                    streak = 0;
                    if (bouquets >= m) return true;
                }
            } else {
                streak = 0;
            }
        }
        return bouquets >= m;
    }
}
```

Since we check need <= n first, answer always exists within [min, max], so lo is valid. Binary search converges to the minimal day.

Time complexity: O(n log(maxDay)) ≈ 10^5 * 30 = 3*10^6 operations. Fine.

