This is the classic regular expression matching problem. I'll solve it with dynamic programming.

Let me define dp[i][j] = true if s[0..i) matches p[0..j).

Base case: dp[0][0] = true (empty string matches empty pattern).

For dp[0][j]: empty string can match patterns like "a*b*c*" — so dp[0][j] = dp[0][j-2] if p[j-1] == '*'.

Transitions:
- If p[j-1] is a normal character or '.':
  - dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] && (p[j-1] == '.' || p[j-1] == s[i-1])
- If p[j-1] == '*':
  - Zero occurrences: dp[i][j] = dp[i][j-2]
  - One or more occurrences: if p[j-2] matches s[i-1] (i.e., p[j-2] == '.' || p[j-2] == s[i-1]), then dp[i][j] = dp[i][j] || dp[i-1][j]

Let me write the code:

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public bool IsMatch(string s, string p) {
        int m = s.Length, n = p.Length;
        bool[,] dp = new bool[m + 1, n + 1];
        dp[0, 0] = true;

        // Handle patterns that can match empty string
        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
            if (p[j - 1] == '*') {
                dp[0, j] = dp[0, j - 2];
            }
        }

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (p[j - 1] == '*') {
                    // Zero occurrences of the preceding element
                    dp[i, j] = dp[i, j - 2];
                    // One or more occurrences
                    if (p[j - 2] == '.' || p[j - 2] == s[i - 1]) {
                        dp[i, j] = dp[i, j] || dp[i - 1, j];
                    }
                } else if (p[j - 1] == '.' || p[j - 1] == s[i - 1]) {
                    dp[i, j] = dp[i - 1, j - 1];
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[m, n];
    }
}
```

