This is the classic "Next Greater Element II" problem. The standard approach is to use a monotonic stack, iterating over the array twice (2n iterations), using modulo to handle the circular nature.

Approach:
- Create result array initialized to -1 (or fill with -1).
- Use a stack of indices. Iterate i from 0 to 2n-1, with actual index = i % n.
- While stack is not empty and nums[stack.Peek()] < nums[i % n], pop and set result[popped] = nums[i % n].
- Push i % n (only if i < n, or just push always — pushing duplicates in second pass is fine but unnecessary; pushing always works too since result is already set).

Let me write the code:

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int[] NextGreaterElements(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.Length;
        int[] result = new int[n];
        System.Array.Fill(result, -1);
        Stack<int> stack = new Stack<int>();
        for (int i = 0; i < 2 * n; i++) {
            int current = nums[i % n];
            while (stack.Count > 0 && nums[stack.Peek()] < current) {
                result[stack.Pop()] = current;
            }
            if (i < n) {
                stack.Push(i);
            }
        }
        return result;
    }
}
```

This handles the case where duplicates exist correctly (strictly greater required). For equal elements, we don't pop since `<` is strict.

