This is the classic "Split Array Largest Sum" problem (LeetCode 410). The standard approach is binary search on the answer.

The idea: the answer lies between max(nums) and sum(nums). For a candidate value `cap`, we can greedily check whether we can split the array into at most `k` subarrays each with sum ≤ cap. If yes, we can try smaller caps; otherwise, we need larger caps.

Binary search:
- lo = max(nums) (each subarray must contain at least one element, so the largest sum is at least the max element)
- hi = sum(nums) (one subarray containing everything)

Feasibility check: greedily accumulate; when adding nums[i] would exceed cap, start a new subarray, count++. If count ≤ k, feasible.

Note: nums[i] can be 0, and k ≤ min(50, nums.length), so k ≤ n, meaning we can always split into k non-empty subarrays. The feasibility check with "at most k" subarrays works because if we can split into fewer than k subarrays with sum ≤ cap, we can always split further (since k ≤ n, we can split into exactly k non-empty subarrays, and splitting only decreases the max sum... wait, splitting a subarray into two parts keeps sums ≤ the original sum, so max sum doesn't increase). So "can split into at most k subarrays with each sum ≤ cap" is equivalent to "can split into exactly k subarrays with each sum ≤ cap" when k ≤ n.

