This is the classic "Random Pick Index" problem (LeetCode 398). The standard approach is reservoir sampling: iterate through the array, and for each index where nums[i] == target, with probability 1/count (where count is the number of matches seen so far), replace the current candidate.

Alternatively, we could precompute a dictionary mapping each value to a list of indices, then pick a random index from the list. Both work. Reservoir sampling uses O(1) extra space but O(n) per pick. With nums.length up to 2*10^4 and 10^4 calls, that's 2*10^8 operations worst case — might be slow but typically acceptable. The dictionary approach uses O(n) space and O(1) per pick, which is safer for time.

Let me use the dictionary approach for efficiency:

```csharp
public class Solution {
    private Dictionary<int, List<int>> map;
    private Random rand;

    public Solution(int[] nums) {
        map = new Dictionary<int, List<int>>();
        rand = new Random();
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.Length; i++) {
            if (!map.TryGetValue(nums[i], out var list)) {
                list = new List<int>();
                map[nums[i]] = list;
            }
            list.Add(i);
        }
    }

    public int Pick(int target) {
        var list = map[target];
        return list[rand.Next(list.Count)];
    }
}
```

This is straightforward. Note: `Random` in C# — creating one instance per Solution object is fine. There could be issues with multiple Solution instances created in quick succession getting the same seed (time-based), but LeetCode's judge typically works fine with this. To be safe, could use a static Random, but instance is fine.

Actually, in newer .NET, `Random()` uses a better seed. LeetCode uses .NET Core / modern C#, so it's fine.

